 number χρ(G) of G is the smallest integer k such that the vertex set\nof G can be partitioned into packings with pairwise diﬀerent widths.\nIt is known that χρ of the square lattice is between 10 and 17.\nWe improve\nthe lower bound to 12. It is also known that for the hexagonal lattice H the\nχρ(H) = 7. On the other hand for six layers of the hexagonal lattice, χρ(H □P6)\nis unbounded. We show that even H □P3 cannot be covered by ﬁnitely many\npackings.\n11--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: William Jockusch (https://dblp.org/pid/49/3911)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago;       * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/jct/Jockusch94 ... The Lower and Upper Bound Problems for Cubical Polytopes.\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/jct/Jockusch94\n\nWilliam Jockusch:\nPerfect Matchings and Perfect Squares. J. Comb. Theory A 67(1): 100-115 (1994)\n  * 1993\n  * Image: Journal Articles\n\n[j1]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/dcg/Jockusch93\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/dcg/Jockusch93\n\nWilliam Jockusch:\nThe Lower and Upper Bound Problems for Cubical Polytopes. Discret. Comput. Geom. 9: 159-163 (1993)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nToggling, rowmotion, and homomesy on interval-closed sets (https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.08520)\nciteturn1academia19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; Our main theorem is an intricate global characterization of rowmotion on interval-closed sets, which we show is equivalent to the toggling definition. ... Finally, we study interval-closed sets of product of chains posets, proving further results about enumeration and homomesy.\nTitle: Toggling, rowmotion, and homomesy on interval-closed sets\nAuthors: Jennifer Elder, Nadia Lafrenière, Erin McNicholas, Jessica Striker, Amanda Welch\nDate: Mon Jul 17 14:30:29 2023\n\nInterval-closed sets of a poset are a natural superset of order ideals. We initiate the study of interval-closed sets of finite posets from enumerative and dynamical perspectives. In particular, we use the generalized toggle group to define rowmotion on interval-closed sets as a product of these toggles. Our main theorem is an intricate global characterization of rowmotion on interval-closed sets, which we show is equivalent to the toggling definition. We also study specific posets; we enumerate interval-closed sets of ordinal sums of antichains, completely describe their rowmotion orbits, and prove a homomesy result involving the signed cardinality statistic. Finally, we study interval-closed sets of product of chains posets, proving further results about enumeration and homomesy.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Polytopes: Abstract, Convex and Computational (https://www.academia.edu/164423432/Polytopes_Abstract_Convex_and_Computational)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; This has been disproved recently by Jockusch, who constructs a sequence of cubical polytopes of increasing dimension for which the ratio of number of facets to number of vertices increases without bound [22]. Jockusch also conjectures a lower bound on fi for cubical polytopes in terms of the number of vertices and the dimension.\n A seemingly basic fact was